  "slug": "cortical-dependencies-18c8",
  "summary": "Harmonized atlases and reanalyses now map injury-state heterogeneity across time and intervention in brain, spinal cord and peripheral nerve.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is single-cell resolution of how glial, myeloid and lymphoid compartments respond to nervous system injury, aging and disease. StrokeTime integrates 21 mouse stroke scRNA-seq studies (414,359 cells) into 120 transcriptional states across post-ischemic stages, and a spinal cord injury reanalysis of three public datasets maps temporally heterogeneous neutrophil-lineage states. Transplantation of human ESC-derived retinal ganglion cells reveals host microglia/macrophage activation that is dynamic and partly reversible. Post-injury nerve macrophages are linked to glycolysis and histone lactylation during myelin clearance. Other items extend the frame to aging human hippocampus, Treg metabolic inflexibility in Moyamoya disease and paraptosis-related genes in diabetic neuropathy. The novelty lies in temporal and intervention-resolved state maps rather than bulk averages.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-24"
}
